<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Opole</h1><p class="st-meta">1281 CE – 1532 CE · capital Opole</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Upper Silesian Piast duchy whose duke Władysław Opolczyk brought the Black Madonna to Częstochowa and whose last prince died in 1532.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1281 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-opole-and-raciborz">Duchy of Opole and Racibórz</a> was divided among the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-of-opole">Władysław of Opole</a>, and Bolko I received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-river">Oder</a>. He accepted Bohemian overlordship in 1327 along with other Upper Silesian dukes, while his descendants continued to rule.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław II of Opole</a> served as palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and in 1382 founded the Pauline monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery">Jasna Góra</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a>, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-madonna-of-czestochowa">Black Madonna</a> icon. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> dukes later gathered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-the-good">John II the Good</a> issued a charter of privileges for the region and allowed the mining town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnowskie-gory">Tarnowskie Góry</a> to develop. The population was largely Polish-speaking.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-the-good">John II</a>'s death in 1532 the Upper Silesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> died out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a> passed to the Bohemian crown under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, pledged at times to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-vasa">Polish Vasas</a>. It went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1742 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-opole.jpg" alt="Duchy of Opole" width="320" height="293"><figcaption>Image: Tymoteusz Trzewiczek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%9Al%C4%85sk_1177-1185.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1281 CE – 1532 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Opole</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bolko I of Opole, Władysław II of Opole, John II the Good</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Oppeln</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1406&amp;focus=duchy-of-opole" aria-label="Duchy of Opole on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5088 419 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Opole lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5408 675L5408 675M5408 675L5330 692M5408 675L5417 692M5408 675L5387 662M5408 675L5417 680"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5408 675h0M5330 692h0M5417 692h0M5387 662h0M5417 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5408 675h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5408 675h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Opole. <a href="/atlas?year=1406&amp;focus=duchy-of-opole">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
